After reading the YANIs in the FAQ, I decided to go for one of them.
I currently have a modified version of NetHack running on my machine,
working (in TTY mode) as far as I can tell after ~an hour of
debugging, with the following changes:

-Potions can not only be Diluted, they can also be Refilled.  Once a
potion becomes Refilled, there is no way to shake this off.
-New item: the Potion of Nothing.  Any potion used up by dipping or
quaffing will produce one of these.  They are always Refilled (even
when wished for), though this status doesn't show until the potion is
dipped or polymorphed into something else.  The Potion of Nothing can
be dipped into any potion but Water to become that type of potion
(this will not replicate potions, only make them Uncursed and Refilled
one at a time).
-A nonmagical refilled potion will never polymorph into a magical
refilled potion.  This can go the other way, however.
-Refilled potions of water are ignored by the gods: they cannot be
blessed/cursed at altars, and do not function as holy/unholy water;
they merely make things wet, regardless of BUC status - and they
provide very little nutrition (only a 50% chance of 1 nutrition when
Uncursed; 1-2 nutrition when Blessed).
-Refilled potions of water will only wet one item at a time from
stacks, so they can't even be used to replicate dips.

Off the top of my head, here are some uses for these empty potion
bottles:
-Storing water for dipping more valuable scrolls/potions when there's
no water around
-Unlimited food source for Foodless players with a ring of slow
digestion (without the ring, they can't keep up with normal hunger
unless repeatedly blessed)
-Polypiling into glass and any sickness/booze that may be needed
-Uncursing more valuable potions (if you don't intend to polypile
them, refilled potions other than water function just as well as their
counterparts) - currently moving potions between bottles clears BUC
status, but I can see arguments for the BUC status moving with the
potion (less useful) or with the bottle (more useful).

I haven't yet downloaded the software for creating patches from my
modified code.


